# Line endings
#
# Store text as LF in the repository AND check out LF in the working tree, on
# every platform.
#
# Why this is pinned rather than left to each machine's git config:
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the common Windows default, and what this repo's
# contributors have set) rewrites files to CRLF on checkout. Biome's formatter
# defaults to LF, so a CRLF working tree fails `bun run check` even though the
# committed content is fine — and because the pre-commit hook runs Biome via
# lint-staged, it then "fixes" files by rewriting their line endings, producing
# large diffs that have nothing to do with the change being made.
#
# Any git operation that rewrites working-tree files can trigger it: stash,
# checkout, branch switch, merge, reset. It is easy to mistake the result for a
# pre-existing formatting problem.
#
# Pinning eol=lf makes Windows behave like Linux and CI, so Biome, the hooks,
# and the repository all agree. It also matters for `.husky/` hooks, which are
# shell scripts that must be LF to execute correctly.
#
# `text=auto` keeps git's binary detection, so the explicit binary rules below
# are belt-and-braces for files git might otherwise sniff as text.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Images
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary

# Fonts
*.otf binary
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.eot binary

# Documents and archives (manuscript fixtures, proposals)
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.zip binary

# Bun's binary lockfile, if this repo ever switches back to it from bun.lock
bun.lockb binary
